# Browse the catalogue

You do not need an account to read the catalogue. Open the catalogue home page,
select a service alias and inspect its published tags, image digests and
platforms. A service alias is a public label; it does not reveal a private
registry path.

## Read the same data over HTTP

Replace the example origin with your catalogue's public origin:

```console
curl --fail https://catalogue.example.test/api/v1/services
curl --fail https://catalogue.example.test/api/v1/services/widget/releases
curl --fail https://catalogue.example.test/api/v1/services/widget/releases/1.2.3
```

The release entry's `version` is the actual upstream container tag. Its `digest`
identifies the OCI image or multi-platform index, and `platforms` lists the
producer's published platform descriptors. `publishedAt` records the first
publication of that tag; `updatedAt` changes when its metadata changes.

Tags can move to another digest unless the producer marked the tag immutable.
A tag called `latest` remains literally `latest`; Release Mirror never replaces
it with a generated version.

## Export public metadata

`GET /api/v1/export` returns a portable JSON snapshot of all public services and
releases. It excludes permissions and private immutability policy. Use a database
backup when you need to preserve the full operational state.

Reads must revalidate cached JSON. Deleting an entry removes it from the live
catalogue but cannot recall copies that readers already downloaded. Deletion
never removes an image from the upstream registry.

See the <%= link_to 'API reference', '/api.html' %> for endpoints and errors, or
<%= link_to 'Connect Renovate', '/renovate.html' %> for automated updates.
